'''David Bronson''' (born June 26, 1958)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Meet Dave|url=https://www.bronsonformayor.com/meet-dave|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=Bronson for Mayor|language=en-US|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522175046/https://www.bronsonformayor.com/meet-dave|url-status=dead}}</ref> is an American politician, pilot, and retired [[United States Air Force|National Guard (Air Force)]] officer. A [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=Herz|first=Nathaniel|date=May 18, 2021|title=Anchorage is trending blue. Here's why it's on track to elect a conservative mayor.|url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/05/17/anchorage-is-trending-blue-heres-why-its-on-track-to-elect-a-conservative-mayor/|url-status=live|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=Alaska Public Media|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210517160808/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/05/17/anchorage-is-trending-blue-heres-why-its-on-track-to-elect-a-conservative-mayor/ |archive-date=May 17, 2021 }}</ref> he was the 37th [[List of mayors of Anchorage, Alaska|mayor]] of [[Anchorage, Alaska]], from 2021 until 2024. Bronson is a candidate in the [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election]].

== Early life and education ==
Bronson was born and raised in the southern end of [[Superior, Wisconsin]]. He was born to parents Bill and Sandy Bronson. He attended Superior High School. As a young boy, Bronson distributed the ''Evening Telegram'' newspaper.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 28, 2021 |title=Superior High School graduate to helm Alaska's biggest city |url=https://www.superiortelegram.com/community/superior-high-school-graduate-to-helm-alaskas-biggest-city |access-date=December 8, 2023 |website=Superior Telegram |language=en}}</ref> He earned a Bachelor of Science degree in agricultural economics from the [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Downing|first=Suzanne|date=August 26, 2020|title=Dave Bronson announces candidacy for mayor of Anchorage to 'turn this city around'|url=https://mustreadalaska.com/dave-bronson-announces-candidacy-for-mayor-of-anchorage-to-turn-this-city-around/|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=Must Read Alaska|language=en-US}}</ref>

== Career ==
After graduating, Bronson was involved in the creation of the Alaska Family Council, a nonprofit organization that promotes [[Social conservatism|traditional]] [[Christian values]] and is against same-sex marriage and abortion.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=George |first=Kavitha |date=June 16, 2021 |title=After bruising campaign, Bronson says he's looking for common ground in Anchorage |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2021/06/16/after-bruising-campaign-mayor-elect-bronson-hopes-governance-means-more-common-ground/ |access-date=December 8, 2023 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref> In 2011, Bronson ran for a Midtown Assembly seat and lost. During the assembly, Bronson was asked about certain policies including collective bargaining and tax burdens.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Spack |first=Kristin |date=March 29, 2011 |title=Running 2011: Assembly Seat G, Midtown Anchorage |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2011/03/29/running-2011-assembly-seat-g-midtown-anchorage/ |access-date=December 8, 2023 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref> Bronson served as a pilot in the [[United States Air Force]] from 1981 to 1990, as an [[Air Force Reserve Command]] plans officer from 1992 to 1993, and an [[Alaska Air National Guard]] maintenance officer and pilot from 1993 to 2005, retiring with the rank of lieutenant colonel.<ref>"Anchorage mayor candidate Q&A: Dave Bronson," ''Anchorage Daily News,'' March 13, 2021. Retrieved September 3, 2022.</ref> He has also worked as a commercial pilot since 1990.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Meet the candidate: Dave Bronson|url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/03/12/meet-the-candidate-dave-bronson/|url-status=live|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=alaskasnewssource.com|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210420125511/https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/03/12/meet-the-candidate-dave-bronson/ |archive-date=April 20, 2021 }}</ref> In January 2021, Bronson declared his candidacy for mayor in the [[2021 Anchorage mayoral election]].<ref>{{Cite web|last1=George|first1=Kavitha|date=January 18, 2021|title=Candidates begin filing for Anchorage municipal office|url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/01/18/candidates-begin-filing-for-anchorage-municipal-office/|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=Alaska Public Media|language=en-US}}</ref> In the general election, Bronson placed first in a field of 15 candidates. Bronson then narrowly defeated Forrest Dunbar in a May 2021 runoff election. Dunbar conceded the race to Bronson on May 21, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|date=May 12, 2021|title=Heated runoff race for Anchorage mayor too close to call|url=https://apnews.com/article/ak-state-wire-anchorage-business-health-coronavirus-pandemic-be6ae33c2f3fe75fc3212f489ab816b2|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=AP NEWS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=May 22, 2021|title=Bronson set to win race for Anchorage mayor; Dunbar concedes|url=https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/anchorage/2021/05/21/bronson-set-to-win-race-for-anchorage-mayor-another-round-of-preliminary-election-results-show/|access-date=May 22, 2021|website=Anchorage Daily News|language=en-US}}</ref> Though Anchorage mayoral elections are nonpartisan, Bronson is a registered Republican.

===Mayor of Anchorage===

Anchorage has [[Mayor–council government|mayor council]] leadership that gives the mayor power similar to that of a chief executive. In other words, Anchorage has a strong mayoral system. In this system, the mayor has veto power as well as the power to propose the annual fiscal budget. However, even though the mayor has the power to propose the budget, the Assembly has the ultimate power to approve or reject proposed changes to the budget. The mayor is also responsible for appointing a municipal manager who is responsible for everyday operations. But, prior to being appointed, the municipal manager must undergo a confirmation process by the city Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Municode Library |url=https://library.municode.com/ak/anchorage/codes/code_of_ordinances |access-date=November 29, 2023 |website=library.municode.com}}</ref>

In October 2023 Bronson proposed a revised operating budget for the city in 2024. Currently, the city of Anchorage spends roughly $600 million annually. Bronson's proposed budget would cut current spending by about $2.4 million. Despite these proposed changes, Bronson has continued to emphasize the importance of core public services such as: increased funding for public transportation, snow removal and road maintenance and repairs. In addition, Bronson emphasized the importance of funding necessary public health and safety services in an attempt to prioritize the needs of Anchorage residents.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hsieh |first=Jeremy |date=October 3, 2023 |title=Anchorage Mayor Bronson proposes leaner budget for 2024 |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2023/10/02/anchorage-mayor-bronson-proposes-leaner-budget-for-2024/ |access-date=November 29, 2023 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cadotte |first=Joe |date=October 3, 2023 |title=Mayor’s 2024 proposed budget shaves off $2.4M from last year’s proposals |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2023/10/03/mayors-2024-proposed-budget-shaves-off-24m-last-years-proposals/ |access-date=November 29, 2023 |website=www.alaskasnewssource.com |language=en}}</ref>

The month after Bronson took office, he revoked a [[paid parental leave]] policy for city workers. Another policy was revoked that allowed non-unionized city workers to bring their young children (up to six months old) to the office. Both policies were enacted by the previous mayor Austin Quinn-Davidson late into her term. Bronson cited the lack of research on the policies prior to implementation and their rushed nature of approval as reason for their dismissal. He also said that he received complaints from workers about children brought to work.<ref>{{cite web | title=Anchorage Mayor Bronson revokes paid parental leave for city workers | website=Alaska Public Media | date=August 1, 2021 | url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/08/31/anchorage-mayor-bronson-revokes-paid-parental-leave-for-city-workers/ | access-date=September 1, 2021}}</ref>

During the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Alaska|COVID-19 pandemic]], Bronson declined to enact [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic|mask]] or vaccine requirements, saying that it was a matter of personal choice. He also said he [[COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy in the United States|would not get a vaccine]], calling it "experimental".<ref>{{Cite news|date=August 1, 2021|title=As COVID-19 cases rise, Anchorage Mayor Bronson says he won't enact restrictions and doesn't plan to get vaccinated|url=https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/anchorage/2021/07/29/as-covid-19-cases-rise-anchorage-mayor-bronson-says-he-wont-enact-restrictions-and-doesnt-plan-to-get-vaccinated/|access-date=August 1, 2021|website=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref> During an [[Anchorage Assembly]] meeting on September 29, 2021, he defended the use of [[Star of David]] [[The Holocaust|holocaust]] imagery worn by those opposed to a municipal mask mandate.<ref name="Goodykoontz">{{cite news |last1=Goodykoontz |first1=Emily |title=Mayor Bronson defends use of Holocaust imagery by mask mandate opponents during heated second night of Anchorage Assembly hearing |url=https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/anchorage/2021/09/29/tensions-run-high-over-proposed-mask-ordinance-at-second-night-of-anchorage-assembly-meeting/ |access-date=September 30, 2021 |work=Anchorage Daily News |date=September 29, 2021}}</ref> The mandatory mask ordinance–requiring any individual older than two years old to wear a facial mask indoors–was debated at the meeting. Bronson initially advocated for the symbol's usage, calling it a "credit to [Jews]," but later recanted the statement after public criticism. He went on to say that although he agreed with opposing the mask ordinance, that protestors should choose a different symbol to do so.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stracqualursi |first=Veronica |date=October 1, 2021 |title=Anchorage mayor apologizes after saying protesters' use of Star of David to oppose mask mandate is 'credit' to Jews {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/01/politics/anchorage-mayor-coronavirus-mask-mandate/index.html |access-date=December 4, 2023 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref>

In May 2023, Bronson closed the [[homeless shelter]] at the [[Sullivan Arena]] leading the homeless encampments in Anchorage parks to "explode in size." Bronson said that the Arena should go back to hosting concerts and hockey games, and suggested sending the homeless people to [[Los Angeles]] and other cities in the [[contiguous United States]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Branson-Potts |first=Hailey |date=August 10, 2023 |title=Ship Alaska's homeless population off to California? They say no way, 'Alaska's my home' |url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2023-08-10/la-me-alaska-anchorage-homeless-los-angeles |access-date=August 10, 2023 |website=Los Angeles Times |language=en-US}}</ref> Bronson had made plans to construct a new shelter for up to 200 people, but the Anchorage Assembly shut down the project. Construction had begun without finalizing funding and without a plan for its operating costs going forward.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Early |first=Wesley |date=August 23, 2023 |title=Assembly rejects putting millions of dollars toward East Anchorage homeless shelter |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2023/08/22/assembly-rejects-putting-millions-of-dollars-toward-east-anchorage-homeless-shelter/ |access-date=December 4, 2023 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Mayoral elections ====
In the [[2021 Anchorage mayoral election]], Bronson and [[Forrest Dunbar]] advanced to a runoff election on May 11 after no one gained a majority in the general election on April 5. Dunbar conceded on May 21. Bronson received 50.66% of the vote, while Dunbar received 49.34%.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mayoral election in Anchorage, Alaska (2021) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Mayoral_election_in_Anchorage,_Alaska_(2021) |access-date=December 8, 2023 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref>

Bronson lost the [[2024 Anchorage mayoral election]] in the May 14 runoff against [[Suzanne LaFrance]].

===2026 gubernatorial campaign===
{{main|2026 Alaska gubernatorial election}}

On October 2, 2025, Bronson announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Barnwell |first1=Jack |publisher=[[Fairbanks Daily News-Miner]] |access-date=July 21, 2026 |url=https://www.newsminer.com/news/alaska_news/dave-bronson-former-anchorage-mayor-enters-governors-race/article_5585e6aa-07f1-433b-b298-03c0b86758af.html |date=October 2, 2025 |title=Dave Bronson, former Anchorage mayor, enters governor's race}}</ref> He received 8.1% of the vote in the [[nonpartisan primary#Alaska|nonpartisan primary]] on August 18, 2026, placing fourth and advancing to the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Samuels |first1= Iris |title= Tom Begich says he will withdraw from Alaska governor's race |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/tom-begich-says-he-will-withdraw-from-alaska-governors-race/ |access-date =August 29, 2026 |work= [[Anchorage Daily News]] |date= August 28, 2026}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Bronson and his wife, Debra, attend Mountain City Church (formerly Anchorage Baptist Temple).<ref>{{Cite web|title=Col. David Bronson – AK Family|url=https://www.akfamily.org/team/david-bronson/|access-date=May 22, 2021|language=en-US|archive-date=May 22, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522175046/https://www.akfamily.org/team/david-bronson/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>"[https://mountaincity.church/new-name/ New Name, Expanding Vision]," ''Mountain City Church,'' 2023. Retrieved June 19, 2024.</ref> During the COVID-19 pandemic, Bronson accepted an early retirement package from [[Delta Air Lines]], where he had been a commercial pilot. Bronson has two children. His son, Zach, is a commercial pilot. Bronson is a hunter and fisherman.<ref name=":0" /> In 2025, he was appointed airport manager of [[Anchorage Airport]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thom |first1=Leah |title=Former Anchorage mayor Bronson takes lead manager job at airport |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/01/18/former-anchorage-mayor-bronson-takes-lead-manager-job-airport/ |access-date=17 July 2025 |work=Alaska's News Source |publisher=KTUU-TV |date=18 January 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

